{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"shiftable-underage-waterdogs","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\nAfter Gerrit change 1560 merged the bounded completion policy, a normal close verification for bug 0707 produced one false unmet criterion. The verifier claimed tests/unit/test_agent_call_seam.py contained only a COMMIT steering notice. The actual merged file contains normal Python imports, test functions, and assertions.\n\n## Proven observation\n\nThe completion wrapper returns COMMIT_STEERING_NOTICE to every losing governed call in a same-response batch (src/rebar/llm/completion_tool_policy.py:153-154). In the observed close run, the model treated that synthetic steering result as if it were the read_file payload and concluded the target file itself was the notice. This is distinct from 0707's zero-bank exhaustion: progress was banked and the close returned a normal FAIL, but evidence fidelity was wrong.\n\n## Plan\n\n- Reproduce with a deterministic or repeated behavioral oracle where a read_file call loses a governed batch and the criterion still requires that file.\n- Distinguish tool-result attribution from general model hallucination by varying only the synthetic result contract.\n- Design an affirmative, schema-compatible steering result that cannot be scored as repository content, preserving all existing tool schemas and completion vocabulary.\n- Prove RED then GREEN and cover primary/successor parity.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A held-out oracle reproduces the verifier treating a suppressed governed call's steering result as repository evidence.\n- [ ] Losing batch calls remain non-executing while their results are unambiguously classified as steering, not file/search/list content.\n- [ ] Completion criteria backed by a real merged file are not marked unmet solely because their concurrent read was suppressed.\n- [ ] Existing finite banking, fallback, schemas, CLI/MCP/library surfaces, workflow/environment names, and old vocabulary remain compatible.\n- [ ] Focused completion tests, live behavioral coverage, lint, typecheck, and full CI pass.","id":"99d3-8618-654b-4f03","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["completion","llm","regression"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Completion verifier can mistake COMMIT steering for repository file content"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786280775808122001,"uuid":"41d1547b-0253-4b30-8d5b-356e927a3445"}